Key novelty identified for the experiment runner:
The user's "sweet spot" hypothesis — that mild preemptive skepticism may improve reasoning
without triggering sycophantic capitulation — is NOT directly tested anywhere in the surveyed
literature. The closest related work treats skepticism as monotonically harmful (sycophancy
literature) or addresses tone separately (EmotionPrompt). A graded preemptive-skepticism
ladder over objective + subjective benchmarks, instrumented with reasoning-token cost and
%Sorry rate alongside accuracy, is the recommended experimental contribution. See §8 of
literature_review.md for the full design recommendation.

Recommended models for the experiment runner:
- GPT-4o-mini (proprietary baseline, used in Sharma/Fanous/Rabbani)
- Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct (open-source instruction-tuned, used in Rabbani/Hong)
- A reasoning-optimised model (o4-mini, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, or Qwen3) to
  measure how the tone effect interacts with explicit-CoT reasoning.

Recommended primary datasets (objective): TruthfulQA-MC1 + MMLU-subset + GSM8K test subset.
Recommended subjective probes: SYCON debate topics + BrokenMath subset.
Recommended baselines: zero-shot, FlipFlop "Are you sure?", EmotionPrompt, Andrew-prompt.
Recommended metrics: accuracy + FlipFlop Δ + flip-rate conditionals + %Sorry + reasoning-token count.
